1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:04,629
An incident lands in ServiceNow at nine at night.

2
00:00:04,629 --> 00:00:07,008
The acknowledgement mail goes out,

3
00:00:07,008 --> 00:00:09,038
and then the questions begin.

4
00:00:09,038 --> 00:00:10,717
Which V I P? Since when?

5
00:00:10,717 --> 00:00:13,167
What changed? Who is looking at it?

6
00:00:13,167 --> 00:00:16,176
And the engineer on call is typing the same

7
00:00:16,176 --> 00:00:19,045
first five commands they typed last week.

8
00:00:19,795 --> 00:00:21,955
What if an agent read the ticket,

9
00:00:21,955 --> 00:00:23,919
asked for the missing details,

10
00:00:23,919 --> 00:00:26,734
pulled the evidence from the load balancer,

11
00:00:26,734 --> 00:00:29,418
and suggested the exact C L I commands to

12
00:00:29,418 --> 00:00:33,346
run next? Troubleshooting, automated, with a person still in

13
00:00:33,346 --> 00:00:34,852
charge of every change.

14
00:00:35,602 --> 00:00:39,902
Gennoor Tech presents Troubleshooting Automation with Claude.

15
00:00:40,652 --> 00:00:43,351
One incident in. One resolution note out.

16
00:00:43,351 --> 00:00:46,906
You build the troubleshooting workflow in ten modules,

17
00:00:46,906 --> 00:00:50,593
and every module ends with something that actually runs.

18
00:00:50,593 --> 00:00:52,766
Claude reads, plans and explains.

19
00:00:52,766 --> 00:00:57,111
Deterministic Python collects the evidence and updates the ticket.

20
00:00:57,111 --> 00:01:00,337
A person approves anything that touches a device.

21
00:01:00,337 --> 00:01:01,983
And every step is logged,

22
00:01:01,983 --> 00:01:05,011
in your audit trail and in the ServiceNow work

23
00:01:05,011 --> 00:01:05,406
notes.

24
00:01:06,156 --> 00:01:09,734
You start with the incident lifecycle in ServiceNow,

25
00:01:09,734 --> 00:01:12,762
from the moment the ticket is created to the

26
00:01:12,762 --> 00:01:16,684
acknowledgement mail and the first troubleshooting reply.

27
00:01:16,684 --> 00:01:19,436
You map where an agent may act and where

28
00:01:19,436 --> 00:01:22,188
it may not. Then you set up Claude Code,

29
00:01:22,188 --> 00:01:24,252
the Claude Agent S D K and M C

30
00:01:24,252 --> 00:01:26,592
P servers for this exact workflow,

31
00:01:26,592 --> 00:01:28,863
with a read only ServiceNow tool,

32
00:01:28,863 --> 00:01:30,514
a read only device tool,

33
00:01:30,514 --> 00:01:33,817
and nothing else. Credentials stay in the vault,

34
00:01:33,817 --> 00:01:35,055
never in a prompt.

35
00:01:35,805 --> 00:01:40,075
Then intake. You pull the incident through the ServiceNow

36
00:01:40,075 --> 00:01:41,948
Table A P I, validate it,

37
00:01:41,948 --> 00:01:46,368
and let Claude compare it against a primary troubleshooting

38
00:01:46,368 --> 00:01:48,316
checklist. Affected V I P,

39
00:01:48,316 --> 00:01:51,313
symptom, start time, scope, last change.

40
00:01:51,313 --> 00:01:54,759
The agent lists what is missing and drafts the

41
00:01:54,759 --> 00:01:58,954
clarification mail. An engineer reviews it and sends it.

42
00:01:59,704 --> 00:02:02,153
Day two is evidence and diagnosis.

43
00:02:02,153 --> 00:02:05,251
Deterministic collectors run F5 t m s h and

44
00:02:05,251 --> 00:02:08,205
iControl REST, the NetScaler NITRO A P I,

45
00:02:08,205 --> 00:02:11,086
Radware Alteon and other load balancers,

46
00:02:11,086 --> 00:02:13,752
and the Cisco A P I C REST interface,

47
00:02:13,752 --> 00:02:16,417
and return typed facts with a device,

48
00:02:16,417 --> 00:02:18,650
a timestamp and the raw output.

49
00:02:18,650 --> 00:02:21,964
Claude turns those facts into a ranked list of

50
00:02:21,964 --> 00:02:25,206
likely causes, and for each cause the exact C

51
00:02:25,206 --> 00:02:28,231
L I command that confirms or rules it out.

52
00:02:28,231 --> 00:02:29,600
Structured J S O N,

53
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:31,905
with a citation for every claim,

54
00:02:31,905 --> 00:02:33,058
never free text.

55
00:02:33,808 --> 00:02:38,095
Then the guardrails. Every suggested command is classified read

56
00:02:38,095 --> 00:02:41,497
only or state changing. Read only commands can run

57
00:02:41,497 --> 00:02:45,036
in the lab automatically. Anything else waits for an

58
00:02:45,036 --> 00:02:47,622
engineer to approve, and the approval,

59
00:02:47,622 --> 00:02:50,684
the command and the output are written to the

60
00:02:50,684 --> 00:02:53,611
audit log and to the ServiceNow work notes.

61
00:02:53,611 --> 00:02:56,673
You will try to push a change through without

62
00:02:56,673 --> 00:02:59,327
approval, and you will watch it refuse.

63
00:02:59,327 --> 00:03:01,164
Then you run the full loop.

64
00:03:01,164 --> 00:03:05,656
Suggest, approve, execute, interpret, until the cause is confirmed

65
00:03:05,656 --> 00:03:07,697
or a senior engineer is paged.

66
00:03:08,447 --> 00:03:10,188
Day three is the template.

67
00:03:10,188 --> 00:03:13,803
Reason, action, next action. Claude drafts it from the

68
00:03:13,803 --> 00:03:17,418
evidence it was given, cites the command output behind

69
00:03:17,418 --> 00:03:20,564
every line, and marks what it could not verify.

70
00:03:20,564 --> 00:03:23,576
You post it to the incident through the Table

71
00:03:23,576 --> 00:03:25,384
A P I, with the close code,

72
00:03:25,384 --> 00:03:28,999
and you create the knowledge article the next incident

73
00:03:28,999 --> 00:03:29,735
will reuse.

74
00:03:30,485 --> 00:03:33,843
You finish by wiring it all into a troubleshooting

75
00:03:33,843 --> 00:03:37,067
page. A FastAPI service with the incident queue,

76
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:39,350
the evidence, the suggested steps,

77
00:03:39,350 --> 00:03:42,641
the approve and reject buttons and the audit tab.

78
00:03:42,641 --> 00:03:45,999
Tested with pytest against recorded device output,

79
00:03:45,999 --> 00:03:49,693
and shipped through a reviewed GitHub pull request with

80
00:03:49,693 --> 00:03:50,633
Actions green.

81
00:03:51,383 --> 00:03:55,057
Every lab runs against a ServiceNow developer instance,

82
00:03:55,057 --> 00:03:57,462
lab load balancers and a Cisco A C I

83
00:03:57,462 --> 00:04:01,336
sandbox, with synthetic incidents that have real symptoms.

84
00:04:01,336 --> 00:04:04,542
You will watch the agent ask the right question,

85
00:04:04,542 --> 00:04:07,748
run the right command, and refuse the wrong one.

86
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:10,821
And in the capstone a fresh incident lands and

87
00:04:10,821 --> 00:04:14,828
your team takes it from acknowledgement to posted resolution

88
00:04:14,828 --> 00:04:15,162
note.

89
00:04:15,912 --> 00:04:18,327
Here is the full table of contents,

90
00:04:18,327 --> 00:04:20,191
10 modules over three days.

91
00:04:20,191 --> 00:04:24,676
Day one: The troubleshooting workflow and the ServiceNow incident

92
00:04:24,676 --> 00:04:27,022
lifecycle; Setting up Claude Code,

93
00:04:27,022 --> 00:04:29,921
the Agent SDK and MCP for incident triage;

94
00:04:29,921 --> 00:04:33,302
and Incident intake through the Table API and the

95
00:04:33,302 --> 00:04:38,132
acknowledgement mail. Day two: Evidence collection from load balancers

96
00:04:38,132 --> 00:04:42,687
and ACI; Claude suggests troubleshooting steps as structured JSON;

97
00:04:42,687 --> 00:04:46,551
Guardrails, approvals and audit for incident automation;

98
00:04:46,551 --> 00:04:50,001
and Running approved steps and reading the result.

99
00:04:50,001 --> 00:04:53,797
Day three: The reason, action and next action template;

100
00:04:53,797 --> 00:04:57,523
The troubleshooting page: service, tests and delivery;

101
00:04:57,523 --> 00:05:00,421
and Capstone: incident to resolution note,

102
00:05:00,421 --> 00:05:01,180
end to end.

103
00:05:01,930 --> 00:05:06,986
Every build in this course follows six engineering principles.

104
00:05:06,986 --> 00:05:09,188
Model output is a proposal,

105
00:05:09,188 --> 00:05:13,428
never a fact. Authorization lives outside the model.

106
00:05:13,428 --> 00:05:17,016
A human approves every consequential action.

107
00:05:17,016 --> 00:05:19,789
Decisions and evidence are logged.

108
00:05:19,789 --> 00:05:24,600
The stack stays provider-agnostic. And quality is measured,

109
00:05:24,600 --> 00:05:25,578
not assumed.

110
00:05:26,328 --> 00:05:29,222
Ten modules that run. Three days,

111
00:05:29,222 --> 00:05:32,027
hands on. Four device platforms.

112
00:05:32,027 --> 00:05:36,586
And zero device changes without a person saying yes.

113
00:05:37,336 --> 00:05:40,317
This course is for network operations,

114
00:05:40,317 --> 00:05:43,454
load balancer and data centre engineers,

115
00:05:43,454 --> 00:05:45,728
N O C and service desk teams,

116
00:05:45,728 --> 00:05:49,493
and I T automation leads in India and worldwide.

117
00:05:49,493 --> 00:05:53,022
You need basic networking, comfort with a C L

118
00:05:53,022 --> 00:05:58,042
I, and some Python. Familiarity with ServiceNow incidents helps,

119
00:05:58,042 --> 00:05:59,846
but it is not required.

120
00:06:00,596 --> 00:06:03,756
Turn every incident into a documented,

121
00:06:03,756 --> 00:06:08,331
repeatable fix. Troubleshooting Automation with Claude.

122
00:06:08,331 --> 00:06:10,744
Enrol now at gennoor dot com.
